# .release-removals — deliberate deletions against the PUBLISHED tree.
#
# One path or path-prefix per line; `#` comments allowed. The `[6/6] public-regress`
# gate blocks any release that removes a file already on public `master` unless it is
# declared here. A deletion is not forbidden — it must be intended, and this file is
# the reviewed record that it was. Read by dev-pipeline/promote/promote.sh.
#
# A comment-only or blank file is NOT an allow-all; it declares nothing.

# ── [ADR-0031], 2026-08-23 — rolling status docs are not a publication surface ──
# `docs/status/STATUS.md` has been public since the last release and its `[[doc]]`
# block is removed from CANONICAL-DOCS.toml in the same commit as this line.
#
# Why: a rolling status file is working memory — dated measurements, seat names,
# blockers, half-verified numbers. The ecosystem pin measurement found 466 of 670
# unreachable commit citations fleet-wide living in exactly these files, and
# sweeping them is the wrong fix because they regenerate the problem by being used
# as intended. Our own STATUS.md already opened by deferring to
# CONFORMANCE-MATRIX.md for every number, which is the argument for this.
#
# What a reader loses: nothing they came for. README.md answers "what is this",
# CHANGELOG.md answers "what changed", CONFORMANCE-MATRIX.md answers "is it
# conformant and against what" — all three declared canonical and written for that
# reader. Reversible: restore the `[[doc]]` block and drop this line.
#
# Prefix, not just the one file: the dated STATUS-*/HANDOFF-*/CHECKPOINT-* snapshots
# beside it are internal too, and none should reach public master again.
docs/status
